<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Federal State of Austria</h1><p class="st-meta">1934 CE – 1938 CE · capital Vienna</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Authoritarian Catholic corporate state in Austria between 1934 and the German annexation of 1938.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss">Chancellor Engelbert Dollfuss</a> suspended parliament in March 1933 and ruled by decree, banning the Communists and Austrian Nazis. After crushing the Social Democrats in the civil war of February 1934, he proclaimed a new constitution on 1 May 1934, creating the Federal State of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> based on Catholic corporatist ideas.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime ruled through a single party, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatherland-front-austria">Fatherland Front</a>, and corporate bodies in place of a parliament, drawing on the papal encyclical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quadragesimo-anno">Quadragesimo Anno</a> and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benito-mussolini">Mussolini's</a> support. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss">Dollfuss</a> was murdered by Austrian Nazis in a failed putsch in July 1934. His successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-schuschnigg">Kurt Schuschnigg</a> tried to preserve independence while yielding to German pressure under the 1936 July Agreement.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In March 1938 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurt-schuschnigg">Schuschnigg</a> called a referendum on independence, but under German threats he resigned. German troops entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> on 12 March 1938, and the country was annexed to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anschluss">Anschluss</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/federal-state-of-austria.jpg" alt="Federal State of Austria" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: R-41 for flag with coat of arms. Original uploader of the coat of arms was David Liuzzo at de.wikipedia · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:State_flag_of_Austria_(1934%E2%80%931938).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1934 CE – 1938 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vienna</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Engelbert Dollfuss, Kurt Schuschnigg, Wilhelm Miklas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Austrofascist Austria, Ständestaat</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=federal-state-of-austria" aria-label="Federal State of Austria on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5061 491 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Federal State of Austria lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5381 747L5381 747M5381 747L5300 622M5381 747L5381 747"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5381 747h0M5300 622h0M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vienna. <a href="/atlas?year=1936&amp;focus=federal-state-of-austria">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
